+// NB: With an embedder's host clock (Embedder/HostClock.h), a timer with a nonzero interval runs on the host's time, as
+//     HTML::Timer does: it is scheduled there instead of on its Core::Timer, which then only holds its interval, kind and
+//     callback. On the wall clock, an embedder that steps its clock by a fixed amount per frame saw these timers (an
+//     animated image's frames, the event loop's 50 ms idle period, timeouts) fire after a number of frames that
+//     depended on how fast the frames ran. A zero interval stays on the Core::Timer: it fires at the event
+//     loop's next pump, whatever the clock. The host's interval is the one the timer was started with; set_interval()
+//     on a running timer takes effect when it is next started.
+static bool runs_on_host_clock(int interval_ms)
+{
+    return interval_ms > 0 && Embedder::host_clock_is_installed();
+}
